takeover time is important to ensure driver safety. The takeover is defined as the combination of physical, visual, and cognitive readiness after TOR is initiated (Zeeb et al., 2015). In most recent studies, the start time of takeover is considered as the time of 10% depression of brake pedal or more than 2◦ rotation of steering wheel by the driver (Zhang et al., 2019; Louw et al., 2017; Zeeb et al., 2015). Post-takeover performance has been evaluated based on time to collision (TTC), acceleration, lane change duration, and time headway to the lead vehicle during the takeover time (Louw et al., 2015; Bueno et al., 2016; Wan & Wu, 2018). Typically, the drivers who showed safer driving behaviors after the TOR warning was generated had longer TTC, lower acceleration, shorter lane change duration, and longer time headway. Thus, these parameters reflect better post-takeover performance. Secondary tasks and modality of TOR significantly affect the takeover time and the post-takeover control (McDonald et al., 2019). Handheld tasks involving personal portable devices (PPD) such as cellphones and tablets will be common secondary tasks during automated driving. However, these handheld secondary tasks have higher adverse effect on both takeover time (Wan & Wu, 2018; Wandtner et al., 2018a) and post-takeover performance (Zeeb et al., 2017; Wandtner et al., 2018a) than non-handheld secondary tasks. This can be because of longer required time for physical and visual readiness (Zhang et al., 2019; Radlmayr et al., 2014; Wan & Wu, 2018; Wandtner et al., 2018a; Zeeb et al., 2017). To reduce the takeover time when drivers use PPD for secondary tasks, the TOR warning can also be provided in PPD in addition to the vehicle interface. In this study, the combination of TOR warning from both PPD and vehicle interface is called “the integrated TOR.” The integrated TOR can display the visual warning to drivers while facilitating their takeover time since the gaze of the driver is already on the PPD screen during the secondary task. In addition to the reduced gaze redirection time from visual and auditory warning, using PPD can also reduce the delay caused by the secondary task. Moreover, listening to auditory warning from PPD using headphone can potentially reduce the time for TOR recognition and visual and physical readiness. Although headphone use should not be permitted for Level 3 driving automation (Gerber et al., 2023), it is worthwhile to test the effects of headphone use on drivers’ response to the integrated TOR as a hypothetical method of improving the auditory warning from PPD. The past studies demonstrated that the integrated TOR warning using PPD can effectively reduce the takeover time and improve the post-takeover performance, particularly when people are engaged in the secondary task using PPD (Melcher et al., 2015; Miller et al., 2015; Politis et al., 2017; Yoon et al., 2019; White et al., 2019). However, these studies neither used auditory TOR warning in PPD nor examined the effects of use of headphone and visual warning that interrupts the video on PPD on takeover. Thus, more studies are needed to evaluate the effectiveness of multimodal (visual and auditory) TOR warning using PPD in reducing the takeover time and improving the post-takeover performance. Thus, the objectives of this study are: (1) to investigate the effectiveness of the integrated TOR in reducing the takeover time and improving the post-takeover performance compared to the conventional TOR that provides the TOR warning in vehicle interface only; and (2) to evaluate the effects of use of headphones during the secondary task on the takeover time and the post-takeover performance. This study will test the following hypotheses that have not been tested in previous research:
Hypothesis 1.1: The integrated TOR reduces the time for visual readiness (i.e., the time it takes to check the TOR warning) compared to the conventional TOR. Hypothesis 1.2: The integrated TOR reduces the time for physical readiness (i.e., the time it takes to be physically ready for manual control) compared to the conventional TOR.
Hypothesis 2.1: The auditory warning through the headphone from PPD and the visual warning that interrupts the video on PPD reduce the take-over time compared to the auditory warning from vehicle interface. Hypothesis 2.2: The auditory warning through the headphone from PPD and the visual warning that interrupts the video on PPD improve the post-takeover performance compared to the auditory warning from vehicle interface. Hypothesis 3: The integrated TOR reduces the drivers’ mental workload.
Hypotheses 2.1 and 2.2 rely on the postulate that auditory warning through the headphone from PPD will improve salience of warnings by blocking ambient sounds and reducing drivers’ cognitive distractions. Hypothesis 3 relies on the postulate that the integrated TOR will increase the drivers’ decision-making ability and level of comfort during the takeover process after the TOR is generated.
2. Literature review
2.1. Effects of secondary task on takeover time and post-takeover performance
The driver’s ability of re-engaging in the driving task depends on the secondary task they perform during autonomous driving. The visual, auditory, physical, and cognitive demands of secondary task affect the efficiency and safety during the vehicle’s transition of control (Zeeb et al., 2016; Marberger et al., 2017). In general, the secondary task significantly increases the driver’s takeover time (Wandtner et al., 2018b; Zeeb et al., 2017; Zhang et al., 2019) and negatively affects the post-takeover performance. On the other hand, the drivers who take longer time to view the driving scene have higher situation awareness to take over (Liang et al., 2021). When drivers are involved in the secondary tasks, it adversely affects the longitudinal post-takeover control and increases chances of crash in higher traffic density (Radlmayr et al., 2014) and decreases the minimum TTC (Gold et al., 2016; Korber et al., 2016). The secondary tasks also affect the post-takeover control, which results in higher lateral acceleration (Louw et al. 2015), higher average and standard deviation of lane position, the lane exceedance (Wandtner et al., 2018b; Zeeb et al., 2016), and longer time and less stability of steering operation (Pan, He, Wang, Cheng, & Dai, 2023; Wu et al., 2020; Zhang, Fard, Davy, Parida, & Robinson, 2023). Furthermore, the secondary tasks increase the time required to change lanes and maximum steering wheel angle (Bueno et al., 2016). In particular, the handheld secondary task using PPD (e.g., phone conversation or trivia game) significantly increases the takeover time (Wan & Wu, 2018). This is mainly because when switching from the task using handheld PPD to take over, the driver takes extra time to decide where to put down the device (Wandtner et al., 2018a; Zeeb et al., 2017). Wan and Wu (2018) showed that watching a video and reading using PPD results in longer reaction time, higher standard deviation of reaction time, and lower TTC relative to other tasks during automated driving. Shi and Bengler (2022) found that the handheld secondary tasks with higher cognitive demand such as playing Tetris on a tablet increased takeover time compared to the task with lower cognitive demand such as watching a video on a laptop. The secondary task using PPD also has negative impacts on posttakeover control. Delayed visual and manual reaction due to the task using PPD causes the driver’s urgent evasive maneuvers instead of a controlled action (Zeeb et al., 2017). Zeeb et al. (2017) also suggested that when PPD was held in hand instead of being mounted, posttakeover performance was degraded.
