2.2. Modalities of takeover request
Different modalities of TOR provided to the drivers greatly influence the takeover time, performance, and quality of the takeover (Naujoks et al., 2014; Petermeijer et al., 2017). Several studies have investigated different alert methods, information provided for successful takeover and types of modality (auditory, visual, vibrotactile, and combination of these modalities). The most common type of modality was the combination of visual and auditory warning (Eriksson et al., 2017; Gold et al., 2015; Miller et al., 2015; Melcher et al., 2015). Naujoks et al. (2014) and Politis et al. (2015) found that multimodal cues are more efficient. Petermeijer et al. (2017) showed that multimodal warning resulted in faster steer-touch time and better usefulness rating and satisfaction among the users. Bazilinskyy et al. (2018) also reported that participants preferred the multimodal TOR warning compared to the unimodal TOR warning. Yun and Yang (2020) found that the multimodal TOR warning showed shorter drivers’ reaction time for TOR and better post-takeover performance than the unimodal (visual only) TOR warning. Different interfaces of the visual warnings were also examined in the previous studies – a mounted screen or a vehicle display (Zeeb et al., 2017; Kaye et al., 2021), a dashboard (Yun & Yang, 2020; Wu et al., 2019; Melcher et al., 2015), a tablet (Yoon et al., 2019; Miller et al., 2015; Politis et al., 2017), and a cellphone (Melcher et al., 2015). In general, audio alerts were generated from the vehicle speakers in the previous studies (Eriksson et al., 2019; Korber et al., 2016; Melcher et al., 2015; Gold et al., 2015; Gold et al., 2016). However, if the driver is involved in the secondary task using PPD and uses a headphone, they may not be able to hear the audio alert from the vehicle speaker.
2.3. Integrated takeover request warning using personal portable device
During the secondary task using PPD, the audio alert from PPD can help reduce the drivers’ takeover time because they can see the warning more quickly and better comprehend the warning message while the secondary task is stopped. For instance, Melcher et al. (2015) integrated the cellphone with the on-screen TOR message in the dashboard by providing the same TOR message on the participants’ cellphone. They compared the participants’ reaction times between the integrated TOR using the cellphone and the TOR without integration of the cellphone (i. e., the on-screen TOR only) using a driving simulator. The participants were asked to perform an artificial secondary task (quiz game) using the cellphone and multimodal (audio and visual) TOR warning was provided for the driving simulator experiment. In this integrated TOR warning, the cellphone only provided the visual alert while the simulator provided the auditory alert. The study suggested that providing 10 s to take over the driving task was sufficient and the integrated TOR warning with the cellphone reduced the mean takeover time. Politis et al. (2017) also conducted a similar study by integrating the TOR using a tablet and playing games as the secondary task. The study provided the visual warning from the tablet but auditory alert using an external speaker. However, the tablet was placed on a stand and the participants did not hold the tablet in their hands. The study found that this integrated TOR warning reduced the takeover time and the lateral deviation. Miller et al. (2015) investigated the effects of different secondary tasks (supervision and media consumption) on the takeover time and the post-takeover performance when the integrated TOR warning was provided in a tablet. They found that there was no significant difference in the drivers’ reaction time and minimum distance headway between supervision and media consumption tasks. In contrast, Yoon et al. (2019) found that different secondary tasks affected the takeover time with the integrated TOR in a tablet. White et al. (2019) observed that the two-stage integrated TOR warning messages in a tablet “Check for hazards” followed by “Resume control” – increased the drivers’ situational awareness before manual control compared to the single TOR warning message of “Resume control.” However, these studies did not
compare the integrated TOR with the conventional TOR. Moreover, similar to Politis et al. (2017), the tablet was located in a fixed position instead of being held by drivers in Yoon et al. (2019) and White et al. (2019).
3. Methodology
3.1. Equipment
To investigate the effects of the integrated TOR on takeover time and post-takeover performance, driver behavior was observed using a fixedbase miniSimTM driving simulator. The simulator consists of three LCD monitors placed within a horizontal field-of-view with two side-view mirrors and a rear-view mirror. Surrounding sound from the speakers and vibration from the driver seat enhance the sense of reality. The auto-drive feature of the miniSim driving simulator (AutoDrive) is capable of longitudinal and lateral vehicle control when the automated driving is activated using the automation button. Automated driving is also capable of overtaking other vehicles and changing lanes. Once the driver moves the steering wheel or presses the brake pedal, the automated driving will stop. Automated driving can also be turned off using the automation button. The status of automated driving is displayed on the screen left to the steering wheel. A Samsung Galaxy S6 Lite tablet with a 10.4-inch display was provided to participants during the experiment. An Android application was developed for the TOR to be provided from the tablet. The tablet was used for the secondary task and the display of TOR warning in the scenarios with the integrated TOR as shown in Fig. 1. In this study, the takeover time is defined as the time from when TOR warning is generated to when the driver is physically ready for manual control (e.g., put hands on steering wheel or put feet on brake/accelerator pedal). To better understand how the integrated TOR can reduce the takeover time, the takeover time was classified into the components of physical and visual readiness time. The takeover time consists of: (1) the TOR Recognition Time (the time it takes to recognize that the TOR warning is generated); (2) the TOR Gaze Redirection Time (the time it takes to check the TOR warning); (3) the Clearance Time (the time it takes to put away the PPD from hands); (4) the Maneuver Gaze Redirection Time (the time it takes to check surrounding environments before manual control); and (5) the Readiness Time (the time it takes to be physically ready for manual control). To measure the physical readiness time for takeover, the times when the driver touched the steering wheel and brake/accelerator pedals were detected using the sensors. The Adafruit extra-long force-sensitive resistor was used for steering wheel and the square force-sensitive
