3.2. Measurement of the components of takeover time
The components of physical and visual readiness time were measured using the data from resistors and video cameras as follows. Since it was not possible to observe when they actually recognized the TOR warning, the TOR Recognition time could not be separately measured. Thus, the TOR Recognition Time was combined with the TOR Gaze Redirection Time for the conventional TOR and the Clearance Time for the integrated TOR. The video cameras recorded the time using Time Stamp Camera Android application. First, the time when TOR was generated (TOR time) in each scenario was recorded from the videos. As shown in Fig. 4, the TOR Gaze Redirection Time (only for the conventional TOR) was measured from the TOR time to the time when facial landmarks indicated that the participant looked forward based on the reference landmark (initial position of participants who are watching a
video). The TOR Gaze Redirection Time was not measured for the integrated TOR because the participants received the TOR warning on the tablet and they did not need to look forward to check the TOR warning in the windshield. The Clearance Time ends when the participants put the tablet on their lap. This time was measured based on the lower right arm angle. The Maneuver Gaze Redirection Time was also measured from the end of the Clearance Time to the time when the participants looked forward based on the reference landmark. The Readiness Time was measured from the end of the Maneuver Gaze Redirection Time to the time when the participants put their hands on the steering wheel or the time when they put their feet on the brake or accelerator pedal, whichever comes before. This end time of the Readiness Time was extracted from the force-sensitive resistors.
