for the participation. The simulator experiment was cleared by the University of Windsor Research Ethics Board. In the driving simulator experiment, the vehicle drove on a four-lane freeway (two lanes in each direction), which was a combination of straight and curved road in normal weather condition. Initially, participants activated automated driving by pressing the automation button. The preset design speed for the experiment was 110 km/hour and the vehicle drove in the right lane. During the automated driving, the participants were engaged in the secondary task (i.e., watch a video on a tablet). Before the experiment, the participants were provided with a tablet with subscribed account of YouTube and Prime video from which they selected their preferred video to watch. The minimum duration of the video selected by the participants was 15 min. Thirty out of the 60 participants used an over ear headphone with noise canceling function (Sony WH-H900N/BM) while watching the video. The participants were informed that they were not required to monitor the driving environment during automated driving. This instruction was provided to the participants to have the participants focus more on the secondary task during the automated driving. In this situation, the effectiveness of the integrated TOR in reducing the takeover time can be better identified. Before the takeover situation, other vehicles simultaneously drove in the same direction as the subject vehicle. The subject vehicle completed the required evasive maneuvers (e.g., changing lanes or slowing down) by itself to safely continue driving. During the automated driving, visual and audio TOR warning was generated in the following two circumstances: (1) the Lane change scenario and (2) the Pullover scenario, as shown in Fig. 5. In both scenarios, the TOR was provided on a straight section of the highway. First, in the Lane change scenario, there was a stopped vehicle in the right lane and the participant received the TOR warning message to change to the left lane to avoid a crash with a time budget of 10 s (i.e., 305 m from the stopped vehicle) and continued driving manually. A 10second time budget was selected for a safe takeover based on the study by Wan and Wu (2018), which observed that crash rate after takeover was significantly low when the time budget was equal or longer than 10 s. They found that drivers did not have enough time to respond to TOR safely when the time budget was shorter than 10 s. For the conventional TOR, only a visual warning message “Change Lane” was displayed in the windshield (i.e., the middle simulator screen) with the auditory warning from the driving simulator. For the Integrated TOR, the same visual warning message was displayed in both windshield and tablet as shown in Fig. 6 with auditory warning from both driving simulator and tablet. The volume of the tablet video was controlled to be the same for all participants and the participants were unable to change the volume by themselves. The volumes of auditory warning at the participant’s position were 60–65 dB and 78–80 dB for the conventional TOR and the integrated TOR, respectively. However, since the difference in volume was small, higher volume of auditorial alert for the integrated TOR is not likely to affect the takeover time and the post-takeover performance. In case of the integrated TOR, at the TOR time, the video was
automatically stopped and the warning message was displayed in the tablet. The participants could press “STOP” button on the tablet to turn off the warning although they were not required to do so. During the takeover situation, there was no surrounding vehicle except the lead stopped vehicle. Second, in the Pullover scenario, the participants were required to pull over to the right shoulder when the TOR was generated. Similar to the Lane change scenario, both conventional and integrated TOR were tested for each participant. In this scenario, a visual warning message “Pull Over to Right” was displayed in the windshield and the tablet. However, there was no surrounding vehicle during the takeover situation. The decision-making procedure after TOR during the two scenarios was different. During the Lane change scenario, once the TOR was generated, the participants were required to analyze the driving environment. They need to make sure that they do not crash with the lead vehicle and there are no vehicles in the target lane to safely change the lane. But during the Pullover scenario, since the participants were only required to pull over the vehicle to the right shoulder, they only need to check that there is no risk of rear-end collision with any following vehicle. Due to additional tasks, the participants need longer takeover time during the Lane change scenario compared to the Pullover scenario. The participants were instructed to put the tablet on their lap before their manual control when the TOR was initiated. In the Lane change scenario after takeover, the participants were instructed to continue driving and after about 500 m of manual driving, the scenario ended. Each participant conducted four driving scenarios as shown in Table 1 (either wearing headphone or not wearing headphone). The participants were informed that they would test these four scenarios, but they were not informed of the order of the scenarios. The order of the scenarios was randomly assigned to each participant to reduce the learning effect. The duration of each scenario was approximately 8–12 min. After testing each scenario, the participants were asked to answer the National Aeronautics and Space Administration Task Load Index (NASA TLX) questionnaire to assess their mental workload after the TOR warning was provided. At the end of the experiment, the participants answered a questionnaire related to their preference and experience of automated driving and TOR.
