end time of experiment. The definitions of these post-takeover performance parameters are shown in Table 2(c). These parameters are explained in more details as follows: TTC has been used as a measure of rear-end collision risk between the lead and following vehicles. A difference in TTC represents meaningful changes in rear-end collision risk only when the possibility of a collision is present as indicated by TTC equal to or shorter than the safety threshold (e.g., 2 s). It is expected that drivers with better posttakeover performance will recognize the surrounding traffic condition earlier and change lanes more safely. Thus, they will change lane faster to avoid a collision with the stopped vehicle and turn a steering wheel more gradually, which results in lower standard deviation of steering wheel angle. In this sense, a shorter TCLC and a smaller standard deviation of steering wheel angle indicate a safer transition from automated to manual driving. However, higher maximum acceleration and deceleration represent a more abrupt change in speed over time, which indicates an unstable transition from automated to manual driving.
3.5. Subjective measurement of mental workload
Mental workload and decision-making have a complex relationship. Decision-making tends to be better for moderate workload, whereas both overload and underload can deteriorate the quality of decision making (Soria-Oliver et al., 2017). After the TOR is generated, taking over the vehicle control is a task that requires a high amplitude of workload. Thus, a reduced mental workload will have a positive effect on the post-takeover performance. In this regard, the NASA TLX was used to provide a reference of the subjective mental workload from different tasks (NASA, 2020). The NASA TLX measures the subjective mental workload using the six different subscales. The first three subscales relate to the demand of task,
which is mental, physical, and temporal demands; the rest of the three subscales relates to impact from the task, which is performance, effort, and frustration. It asks the user to provide a separate subjective rating based on these subscales. The participants completed the NASA TLX questionnaire using the tablet.
3.6. Preference and experience for automated driving and TOR warning
The survey was conducted to ask the participants about their experience and preference for automated driving and TOR warning as shown in Fig. 7.
3.7. Generalized linear models
Various driving performance parameters during the post-takeover situation were extracted from the driving simulator using Python and R programming language. The data from the participants who crashed or missed the TOR warning were excluded from the analysis. This is because in these cases, the participants could not complete manual control and their takeover time and post-takeover performance could not be observed. Statistical analysis was performed using Statistical Analysis Software (SAS) version 9.4. Generalized linear models (GLM) were developed to identify the relationship between a dependent variable (takeover time, TTC, TCLC, standard deviation of steering wheel angle and the subjective mental workload value from NASA TLX) and independent variables (type of TOR warning (integrated or conventional), type of scenario (Lane change or Pullover), the use of headphone, age, gender and driving experience). GLM was used because the model builds a linear relationship between the independent and dependent variables and it can fit to any distribution of the dependent variable, not only the normal distribution. The model can also analyze effects of continuous and categorical variables on a discrete or continuous dependent variable. The scenarios when the participants crashed (Lane change scenario) or missed the TOR completely (Pullover scenario) were excluded. In particular, the functional form of GLM ensures that the dependent variable is a non-negative value as follows:
ln(y) = a + Σi bi xi
where y = dependent variable, xi = independent variable i, a = constant, and bi = coefficient for independent variable i. Since the takeover time, the post-takeover performance parameters, and the subject mental workload are non-negative values, GLM is suitable for predicting these as dependent variables.
4. Results
4.1. Descriptive statistics
Table 3 shows the descriptive statistics of the takeover time, the posttakeover performance parameters, and the subjective mental workload in different scenarios. The significance of the difference in mean of each variable between the integrated and conventional TOR was assessed using two-sample t-tests at a 95% confidence interval. Table 3(a) shows that the integrated TOR has significantly shorter mean takeover time than the conventional TOR with or without the headphone in both scenario types (p-value < 0.05), except for the case without the headphone in the Pullover scenario (p-value = 0.29). This is similar to the findings in Melcher et al. (2015) and Politis et al. (2017). Table 3(b) compares TTC between the conventional and integrated TOR for the Lane change scenario only, because of the absence of a lead vehicle in the Pullover scenario. The table shows that the average value of minimum TTC was significantly longer for the integrated TOR than the conventional TOR with or without the headphone (p-value < 0.05).
