4.2. Components of visual and physical readiness time
Table 4 shows that the components of visual and physical readiness time measured using force-sensitive sensors and cameras. Due to a small number of observations (i.e., 6 to 8 observations for each scenario), the differences in the components between the conventional and integrated TOR were assessed using two non-parametric statistical tests (MannWhitney U test and bootstrapping test). The differences in medians (Mann-Whitney U test) and means (bootstrapping test) between the conventional and integrated TOR were assessed at a 95% confidence interval. The results of both tests show that when the headphone was not used, the Clearance Time was significantly longer for the integrated TOR than the conventional TOR in the Lane change scenario at a 95% confidence interval. This is mainly because the additional time was required to read the warning from the tablet in the integrated TOR. Also, the result of the bootstrapping test shows that when the headphone was used, the Readiness Time was significantly shorter for
the integrated TOR than the conventional TOR in the Lane change scenario at a 95% confidence interval. On the other hand, the Readiness Time was rather longer for the integrated TOR in the Pullover scenario. This indicates that the integrated TOR helped drivers take control faster in more urgent condition when the driver intervention (i.e., lane change to avoid a collision with the lead vehicle) is immediately required. However, due to absence of the TOR Gaze Redirection Time as shown in Fig. 4, the takeover process was faster for the integrated TOR than the conventional TOR in both scenarios.
4.3. Generalized linear models
GLMs were estimated to identify the relationship between the takeover time and the related factors as shown in Table 5(a). GLMs were also estimated to identify the relationship between the post-takeover performance parameters and the related factors as shown in Table 5(b). In both scenarios, the effects of the integrated TOR were significant at a 95% confidence interval (p-value < 0.05). However, the goodness-of-fit
of GLMs for the Pullover scenario was low (R2 = 0.09 – 0.11) compared to the Lane change scenario.
