5. Discussion
Based on the comparison of components of visual and physical readiness time, the reason for why the integrated TOR can reduce the takeover time was identified as follows. This is because the integrated TOR can can save the time it takes for the drivers to redirect their gaze to read the TOR warning message on the dashboard or windshield, not necessarily because it can reduce both visual readiness time and physical readiness time. However, the integrated TOR did not consistently reduce the times it took for the drivers to check the TOR warning and be physically ready for manual driving in both Lane change and Pullover scenarios. Thus, the Hypotheses 1.1 and 1.2 are rejected. Based on the result of GLMs, the factors associated with the takeover time were discussed as follows. In the Lane change scenario, the takeover time was relatively shorter for male participants than female participants. This result is consistent with Lipps et al. (2011), which found that male drivers generally showed shorter reaction time than female drivers. Also, the takeover time was shorter for the participants with longer driving experience. This result is consistent with the fact that the drivers with longer driving experience have quicker driving-related reflexes. For instance, Wright et al. (2016) found that more experienced middle-aged drivers visually identified hazardous situations quicker compared to less experienced drivers. The use of headphones increased the takeover time. This result is similar to the study by de Waard et al. (2011), which found that listening to music using earbuds during cycling increased the cyclists’ response time to the auditory information. On the other hand, this is not the expected effect for the integrated TOR because the drivers can hear louder and more salient auditory TOR warning from headphones. However, the interaction effect of the integrated TOR and the headphone use on the takeover time was not significant. This indicates that the auditory TOR warning from PPD through headphones in the integrated TOR did not significantly reduce the time for TOR recognition and visual and physical readiness. This finding contradicts the hypothesis that the use of headphones will reduce drivers’ cognitive distractions and help them recognize the TOR warning faster. Thus, Hypothesis 2.1 is rejected. This is potentially because the drivers could still recognize the warning faster even without using headphones as the video was automatically stopped and the visual TOR warning was displayed on the tablet. In the Pullover scenario, only the integrated TOR showed significant negative effect on the takeover time. There were no outliers of the
takeover time for the Pullover scenario. The R2 value of the model (= 0.09) was low because the impact of the TOR warning on the takeover time was relatively less significant when the drivers were not required to urgently react to avoid a crash and the takeover situation was relatively safer. Based on the result of GLMs, the factors associated with the posttakeover performance were also discussed as follows. In the Lane change scenario, the integrated TOR significantly increased TTC. This indicates that the integrated TOR can increase the safety margin to avoid a collision. The integrated TOR also decreased standard deviation of steering wheel angle and TCLC, which represent better post-takeover performance. However, the use of headphones significantly reduced TTC while increasing the standard deviation of steering wheel angle and TCLC. This indicates that the use of headphones generally decreased the posttakeover performance for both integrated and conventional TOR. This is similar to the findings in de Waard et al. (2011). Moreover, there was no significant interaction effect of the type of TOR warning and the use of headphones. This indicates that the auditory TOR warning from PPD through headphones did not significantly improve the post-takeover performance. Thus, Hypothesis 2.2 is rejected. The result also shows that in the Pullover scenario, the integrated
TOR significantly reduced the standard deviation of steering wheel angle but increased the maximum deceleration. Also, the maximum deceleration was lower for older drivers. Overall, the signs of coefficients of the ‘Integrated TOR’ variable in the GLMs indicate that the integrated TOR generally improved post-takeover performance compared to the conventional TOR. Politis et al. (2017) also found positive effect of the integrated TOR on the post-takeover performance the reduction in lateral deviation after takeover. GLMs were also estimated for the subjective mental workload but the
model fit was very low (R2 = 0.04 for the Lane change scenario and R2 = 0.09 for the Pullover scenario). Due to an insignificant difference in the effects on the subjective mental workload between the integrated and conventional TOR, Hypothesis 3 is rejected. Lastly, the survey shows the participants’ preference and experience for automated driving and TOR warning. Based on their responses, the participants generally felt higher levels of comfort and safety level with the integrated TOR than the conventional TOR.
6. Conclusions and recommendations
This study investigated the effects of integrating takeover request (TOR) warning with a personal portable device (PPD) called the “integrated TOR” on takeover time and post-takeover performance in level 3 automated driving by comparing with the conventional TOR warning without a PPD. Main findings of this study are summarized as follows: First, the integrated TOR significantly reduced average takeover time compared to the conventional TOR. It was found from the force-sensitive resistor and camera data that the integrated TOR could reduce the takeover time mainly because it eliminated the time required to redirect the driver’s gaze to read the TOR warning message on the dashboard or windshield. Moreover, as the video was automatically stopped (i.e., the drivers could not perform the secondary task) after the TOR warning was generated, the drivers could respond to the TOR faster. Second, the participants generally showed better post-takeover performance with the integrated TOR than the conventional TOR. The integrated TOR increased the minimum time-to-collision (TTC) and decreased the time to complete lane change after manual control (TCLC) in the Lane Change scenario where the participants were requested to change lane to avoid a collision with the stopped vehicle. Also, the integrated TOR decreased the standard deviation of steering wheel angle in both Lane Change scenario and Pullover scenario where the participants were requested to pull over to the right shoulder. As a result, there was no crash with the integrated TOR unlike the conventional TOR. Third, the use of headphones during the secondary task generally increased the takeover time and decreased the post-takeover performance in the Lane change scenario. Thus, the auditory TOR warning from PPD in the integrated TOR did not effectively reduce the time for TOR recognition and visual and physical readiness. This is potentially because the drivers could recognize the visual TOR warning from the tablet faster even without headphones after the video was automatically stopped. Fourth, the impacts of TOR warning on the takeover time and the post takeover-performance were more significant in more urgent takeover situation (Lane change scenario) than less urgent takeover situation (Pullover scenario). Fifth, the average value of subjective mental workload was lower for the integrated TOR than the conventional TOR for both Lane change and Pullover scenarios. This shows that the integrated TOR can improve drivers’ comfort during the takeover process. Lastly, the participants preferred the integrated TOR over the conventional TOR due to higher subjective safety and comfort levels. In summary, a unique finding in this study is that the integrated TOR in PPD can significantly reduce the takeover time and improve the posttakeover performance in both urgent and less urgent conditions. However, the finding shows that the use of headphones for this integrated TOR did not significantly reduce the takeover time or improve the post
