takeover performance. Due to this limitation and the restriction of headphone use for Level 3 driving automation, headphone use is not recommended for listening to auditorial warning from PPD. However, the study has several limitations. First, the difference in the results between the integrated and conventional TOR might have occurred not only due to different location of TOR, but also different visual design of the alerts (e.g., color and size of text) between PPD and vehicle interface. Thus, sole effect of location of TOR could not be identified in this study. Second, there is a limitation of ecological validity of the driving simulator scenario. For instance, the design speed of 110 km/h, which was selected based on a typical speed limit on a limited-access highway in Canada, may be too high for safe takeover of SAE Level 3 vehicles. Thus, it is recommended to conduct the study using lower operating speed to simulate more realistic conditions for takeover. Third, most participants were in a younger age group and there were more male participants than female participants. Lastly, the study could not observe the materials the participants watched on the tablet, which could have affected their level of concentration on the secondary task and their reaction to the TOR warning. In future studies, it is recommended to conduct the experiment for participants with a wider range of age, driving experience, and equal number of male and female participants for generalized results. It is also recommended to evaluate the effectiveness of the integrated TOR in different road geometry, traffic, and weather conditions while participants engage in different types of secondary tasks.
